WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.160
This episode is brought to you by the Music
for Scientists album, now available on all

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.944
streaming services

00:00:13.323 --> 00:00:20.430
When it comes to making more of themselves, single-celled eukaryotes—or protists—have a few options.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:25.340
They can go the asexual reproduction route,
dividing and making nearly exact copies of

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.340
themselves.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.750
And this is a pretty nice way of life.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.320
The euglenoids have taken to it so strongly
that they’ve never been observed reproducing

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.320
any other way.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.360
I mean, think of the advantages.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.320
You don’t have to waste time trying to find
a mate.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.809
And when the process is done, the end result
is more of yourself.

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:50.920
Now, that might sound narcissistic, but when
it comes to survival, it’s also pragmatic.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.990
If your genes work, why not just make more
of them?

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:00.070
But the world is always changing and testing
the organisms within it.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:06.000
Temperatures can become uncomfortable, nutrients
can become scarce, and enemies can become

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.200
plentiful.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.670
Adapting to these changing conditions requires
change in the organism and its progeny.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:20.290
That change can come about in many different
ways and manifest in many different forms,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.970
but one of the tried and true methods is sexual
reproduction.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:29.170
The principle seems straightforward enough,
whether you’re talking about protists or

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.840
plants or people.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.810
You take two parents, mix and match their
genes, and produce a set of offspring with

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:42.270
the hopes that they will better survive the
hardships both the present and the future.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:48.680
Of course, in practice, sexual reproduction
takes on many different forms, some of which

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.710
we’ve documented and discussed already on
this channel.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:58.100
Today we’re going to focus on one particular
method of ciliate reproduction: my personal

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.170
favorite, conjugation.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:05.080
We’ve actually shown ciliates like these
mid-coitus before.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.640
Sometimes they seem to be frantically chasing
each other like these two are.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:16.450
And sometimes, they’re more chill, like
two blobs that are just very, very…close.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:21.980
To explain what makes this specifically conjugation,
we need to first take a moment to explain

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.060
a different kind of sexual reproduction.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:31.560
For some protists, like Chlamydomonas, sexual
reproduction is a fusion of two gamete cells.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.570
It will spend much of its life as an asexual
cell, reproducing through binary fission,

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.610
or just splitting in two.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:46.050
But when the environment gets tough, it will
turn into a gamete that belongs in one of

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.880
two mating types.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.850
When a gamete finds its opposite mating type,
the two fuse to create a zygote that then

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.770
divides to create the progeny.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:02.190
Conjugation, in contrast, is not the fusion
of two gamete cells.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:07.110
It is the fusion of gametic nuclei.

00:03:09.462 --> 00:03:12.332
What is that?

00:03:12.778 --> 00:03:19.808
Well, when you watch ciliates mid-conjugation,
you are watching two individuals that have

00:03:19.808 --> 00:03:28.280
fused together, but it’s a temporary connection
forged by a mutual desire for the other’s nuclei.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.269
Now, ciliates have two different kinds of
nuclei: the macronucleus and the micronucleus.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:38.820
The macronucleus works like our own, housing
the genetic template that gets transcribed

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:43.220
and translated into the molecules the organism
needs for its daily life.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:49.080
But in conjugation, the micronucleus becomes
the dominant player, dividing through meiosis

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:56.900
to create a series of pronuclei that contain
a single set of the organism’s genetic material—though

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.900
all but one of the pronuclei will get promptly
destroyed.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:07.580
The one that remains divides by mitosis so
that the organism now has two identical copies

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:12.580
of the pronuclei: one for itself, and one
for its partner.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:19.810
In the meantime, their original macronucleus
will fall apart, which would normally be catastrophic.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.310
But in this case, it is deliberate.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:27.900
The two ciliates will then exchange one of
their pronuclei with the other through their

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:35.150
connected mouths, and each will fuse their
pronucleus with their partner’s to create

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.560
a zygotic nucleus.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:46.350
This new nucleus combines and rearranges the
genetic material inherited from both parents,

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:53.229
replacing the old macronucleus with this new
remixed version.

00:04:53.229 --> 00:05:01.280
So instead of having sex and producing offspring,
these two ciliates have sex and BECOME the

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.280
offspring.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:09.440
They are both GENETICALLY DIFFERENT after
the conjugation than they were before.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.060
When they’re done, the conjugants separate,
and to pass those new genes on to another

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:20.280
generation, the organisms will have to eventually
divide, producing daughter cells that now

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.669
carry their own copies of this new nucleus.

00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:30.030
So what sets the mood for conjugation?

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.009
Well, it can be a few things.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:36.440
For Paramecium tetraurelia, it can be a matter
of age.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.910
Scientists have documented that Paramecium
tetraurelia can only survive around 200 rounds

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:48.130
of asexual reproduction unless it undergoes
self-fertilization or conjugation to patch

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:53.140
up the genetic damage that can accumulate
over multiple rounds of division.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:58.940
Scientists have also observed that a lack
of nutrients can set other organisms off to

00:05:58.940 --> 00:05:59.940
conjugate.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:07.410
But of course, environmental or aging considerations
aside, the most vital step is just finding

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:08.930
a partner.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.960
Think of how difficult this must be for microbes.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:17.550
They’ve got to navigate a world of debris
and predators without sound or sight or any

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:22.300
of the large scale mating displays you often
find in the macro world.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.610
And even if you do find someone to mate with,
they might be a bit occupied.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.870
Like these two hypotrich ciliates that are
trying so hard to rudely interrupt a couple

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.490
that is already mid-conjugation.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:41.320
They are expending so much effort, though
the solution to their predicament seems so

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:48.770
obvious: they should just channel that energy
into conjugating with each other.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:52.400
So why are they so preoccupied with a couple
that’s already taken?

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.250
Well, for one possible answer, let’s look
at a different ciliate: the Blepharisma.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:04.819
Within a few hours of being starved, sexually
mature Blepharisma will pair up and begin

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:05.819
conjugation.

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:08.970
But the process is more than just stumbling
around the microcosmos.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:14.990
Blepharisma come in two mating types, called
mating-type I and mating-type II, which will

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.169
only mate with the opposite type.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:21.790
And to find their partners, Blepharisma rely
on chemical signals.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:26.810
When Type I Blepharisma are starved, they
begin to secrete a chemical called gamone

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:33.270
1, which alerts any nearby Type II Blepharisma
to the fact that there are some Type I’s

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.259
waiting around and interested.

00:07:35.259 --> 00:07:41.190
The Type II cells transform and release a
chemical called…yes…gamone 2, which then

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.960
finds its way to the Type I cells.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:49.290
These gamones do more than help the different
mating types find each other, they act upon

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:55.379
them to get them ready for conjugation, kind
of like microscopic flirting with a hidden

00:07:55.379 --> 00:07:57.349
chemical language.

00:07:57.349 --> 00:08:02.350
For our poor unloved hypotrich ciliates, what’s
likely happened is that they are the same

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:07.759
mating type as each other, drawn to the same
location because of the secretions of one

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:09.419
of the conjugating pair.

00:08:09.419 --> 00:08:14.860
But alas, they both showed up too late to
the party, and they’ve remained too chemically

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:21.840
preoccupied with the object of their conjugational
affections to divert their attentions to each other.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.230
So while conjugation may be advantageous for
the species, it doesn’t always work out

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:28.690
so well for the individuals.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:33.959
But as always with biology, the underlying
mechanisms are often more mysterious than

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:36.990
the simple image we originally lay out.

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:42.169
Blepharisma and other ciliates with mating
types can sometimes spontaneously switch their

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:47.750
mating type, though we don’t fully understand
how or why this happens.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:53.700
This is but one moment in these hypotrich’s
attempt at conjugation, who knows what the

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:58.410
next one will look like
And as for the conjugating pair, well, we

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.630
wish them and their progeny the best.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:06.580
Because when the camera is gone and the day
is done, these two organisms may never see

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.470
each other again.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:14.930
But they’ve shared this moment and their
nuclei, and they’ve made the microcosmos

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.162
all the richer for it.

00:09:26.839 --> 00:09:33.080
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

